		&lt;li style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;fourth sentence&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;T.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;It starts talking about a condition. In the previous sentence, it is mentioned that&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;the Chinese government has been prompted to take action against wildlife trade&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;and sentence&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;starts&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;predicting the result of such actions.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;last sentence&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;Q&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;which is clearly the&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;continuation of&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;and concludes the paragraph.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px 0px 10px; color:  (51, 51, 51); font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; background-color:  (255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;&lt;em style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the correct chronological order of the passage is&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;SRPTQ.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is &lt;strong&gt;option 5), i.e.&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;u&gt;Q.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;
	&lt;li style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;The passage is about&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;the world&amp;#39;s most trafficked animal, Pangolin&amp;nbsp;&lt;/u&gt;and it also mentions that&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;the existence of this animal is in danger.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;We must go through the sentences in the following ways:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;
	&lt;ul style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin-top: 0px; margin-bottom: 0px;&quot;&gt;
		&lt;li style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;The passage should&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;begin&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;with sentence&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;S&lt;/span&gt;. In this sentence,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;the subject matter is clearly mentioned&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;and thus,&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;it is introducing the topic&lt;/u&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;second sentence&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;should be&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;R&lt;/span&gt;. Here it is mentioned that&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;these animals have been associated or linked to&amp;nbsp;COVID-19&lt;/u&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;next sentence&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;P&lt;/span&gt;. This sentence talks about&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;the evidence of the fact which is mentioned in the previous sentence&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;and also mentions&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;the consequence of the fact&lt;/u&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;fourth sentence&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;T.&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;It starts talking about a condition. In the previous sentence, it is mentioned that&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;the Chinese government has been prompted to take action against wildlife trade&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;and sentence&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;starts&amp;nbsp;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;predicting the result of such actions.&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;last sentence&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;Q&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;which is clearly the&amp;nbsp;&lt;u&gt;continuation of&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;T&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;and concludes the paragraph.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; margin: 0px 0px 10px; color: rgb(51, 51, 51); font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif; font-size: 14px; background-color: rgb(255, 255, 255);&quot;&gt;&lt;u style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;&lt;em style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the correct chronological order of the passage is&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;box-sizing: border-box; font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;SRPTQ.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is &lt;strong&gt;option 1)&lt;/strong&gt; i.e. &lt;strong&gt;as big as yours.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span id=&quot;docs-internal-guid-e70feeff-7fff-da80-af7c-83e7701568e0&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial; color: (0, 0, 0);  font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;border:none;display:inline-block;overflow:hidden;width:40px;height:35px;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;35&quot; src=&quot;https://lh5.googleusercontent.com/28i2H4qZUMqoricKhH6aaLqxFoqVxqpmaJpMBy62DoGJbWBmmYJxPuBF6Wq_gGqfYCkpEpr0WIm9NnvtUCdNGqm-1_wqVyF46346wBwUiFK7AiZX8LxkL7abs3B0GOqVQ9Wwde-d&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;margin-top:0px;&quot; width=&quot;40&quot; /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10.5pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, sans-serif;  font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt;Explanation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;comparative degree&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;#39;bigger&amp;#39; is used in the sentence just after the noun &amp;#39;&lt;strong&gt;times&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;#39;. This is incorrect.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;We&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: 700;&quot;&gt;cannot use a comparative adjective&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;b&gt;after a noun.&lt;/b&gt;&amp;nbsp;It can only be used after a verb. Therefore, &amp;#39;bigger than yours&amp;#39; has to be replaced with &amp;#39;as big as yours&amp;#39;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hence&lt;/strong&gt;, &amp;quot;&lt;em&gt;My palace is five times&lt;/em&gt; &lt;strong&gt;&lt;em&gt;as big as yours&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;quot; is the &lt;strong&gt;correct&lt;/strong&gt; answer.&lt;br /&gt;
	&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span id=&quot;docs-internal-guid-319dda7a-7fff-cdc1-7ff7-9bdb9e2687ea&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10.5pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, sans-serif;  font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;border:none;display:inline-block;overflow:hidden;width:120px;height:40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;40&quot; src=&quot;https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/sWn1oC6mlNsROP-zVXMvAlT_Fqes0HlQMQeDtmr94LuJqLv7dOdQtJ2Q7iQ1JS5REaHqKFCFChrBmdKT4svOFMwg4y1JdWWC5pg4X0ls_aEdE-_EV4rBZGTOua1aQGpEgOl5QU9H&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;margin-top:0px;&quot; width=&quot;120&quot; /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10.5pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, sans-serif;  font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp; &amp;nbsp; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;comparative degree&lt;/strong&gt; is the form of an adverb or adjective modified by more or ending in -er that is used when comparing two things. It is used in this pattern:&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 14px;&quot;&gt;Noun (subject) + verb + comparative adjective + than + noun (object).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The correct answer is &lt;strong&gt;option 3)&lt;/strong&gt; i.e. &lt;strong&gt;too weak to&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;br /&gt;
&lt;span id=&quot;docs-internal-guid-52f3764a-7fff-f4de-f215-207183e04394&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 11pt; font-family: Arial; color: rgb(0, 0, 0); background-color: transparent; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;border:none;display:inline-block;overflow:hidden;width:40px;height:35px;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;35&quot; src=&quot;https://lh5.googleusercontent.com/28i2H4qZUMqoricKhH6aaLqxFoqVxqpmaJpMBy62DoGJbWBmmYJxPuBF6Wq_gGqfYCkpEpr0WIm9NnvtUCdNGqm-1_wqVyF46346wBwUiFK7AiZX8LxkL7abs3B0GOqVQ9Wwde-d&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;margin-top:0px;&quot; width=&quot;40&quot; /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10.5pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt;Explanation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;​We use &amp;#39;&lt;strong&gt;too&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;#39; always before the&amp;nbsp;&lt;strong&gt;positive degree.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;In the given sentence, &amp;#39;&lt;strong&gt;weak&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;#39; should be the &lt;strong&gt;positive degree&lt;/strong&gt;. It is grammatically incorrect to use any other degrees after &amp;#39;too&amp;#39;.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;structure&lt;/strong&gt; for &lt;strong&gt;&amp;#39;too....to&amp;#39;&lt;/strong&gt;:
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;Too+ positive degree+ to+ verb.
		&lt;ul&gt;
			&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Example&lt;/strong&gt;: &lt;em&gt;It was too cold to go out.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;/ul&gt;
		&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hence&lt;/strong&gt;, &amp;quot;&lt;em&gt;She was &lt;strong&gt;too weak&amp;nbsp;to&lt;/strong&gt; walk&lt;/em&gt;&amp;quot; is the correct answer.&lt;br /&gt;
	&amp;nbsp;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;span id=&quot;docs-internal-guid-e1da27c7-7fff-7879-40d5-95a86a6d1a7b&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10.5pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;border:none;display:inline-block;overflow:hidden;width:120px;height:40px;&quot;&gt;&lt;img height=&quot;40&quot; src=&quot;https://lh3.googleusercontent.com/sWn1oC6mlNsROP-zVXMvAlT_Fqes0HlQMQeDtmr94LuJqLv7dOdQtJ2Q7iQ1JS5REaHqKFCFChrBmdKT4svOFMwg4y1JdWWC5pg4X0ls_aEdE-_EV4rBZGTOua1aQGpEgOl5QU9H&quot; style=&quot;margin-left:0px;margin-top:0px;&quot; width=&quot;120&quot; /&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-size: 10.5pt; font-family: &amp;quot;Helvetica Neue&amp;quot;, sans-serif; background-color: transparent; font-weight: 700; font-variant-numeric: normal; font-variant-east-asian: normal; vertical-align: baseline; white-space: pre-wrap;&quot;&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The&lt;strong&gt; Positive degree&lt;/strong&gt; is a term that relates to adjectives and adverbs. An adjective or adverb that does not make a comparison is said to be in the positive degree. (In other words, the &lt;strong&gt;&amp;quot;positive degree&amp;quot; is the normal form of an adjective or adverb&lt;/strong&gt;.)&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;The positive degree of an adjective or adverb offers no comparison. &lt;strong&gt;For example:&lt;/strong&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;adjectives:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;rich, pretty, handsome, good.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;adverbs:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;em&gt;slowly, beautifully.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The alphabets are positioned in the following order:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/20/09/Common_Diagram_28.01.2020_D1.png&quot; style=&quot;width: 536px; height: 128px;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The word can be represented as follows:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In forward direction: &amp;ldquo;EG&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In backward direction: &amp;ldquo;EG&amp;rdquo;, &amp;ldquo;NR&amp;rdquo;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;img alt=&quot;&quot; src=&quot;//storage.googleapis.com/tb-img/production/21/09/5f5a0b3c7a08826e94055aad_16311046176551.png&quot; style=&quot;&quot; /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thus, there are three pairs of letters in the word &amp;ldquo;FOREIGNER&amp;rdquo; as according to the English alphabetical series.&lt;/p&gt;
Hence, &lt;strong&gt;three&lt;/strong&gt; is the correct answer.

